# day 13 · piece 6 of 6 — pattern-bre.txt: the same regex in the OTHER dialect
#
# This piece is a one-line text file, not a script: the BASIC-regex spelling of the
# extended pattern ^(ab|cd)+x?$. In BRE, ( ) | + ? are literal characters unless
# backslashed: \( \) \| \+ \?. The #RUN feeds probe strings through `grep -f` with YOUR
# pattern (no -E) — ab, cd, abcd, abx and cdcdx should match; x, abc and axb should not.
# The skeleton is the ERE spelling, which as a BRE matches only the literal text.
#DAY 13
#FILE pattern-bre.txt
#RUN printf '%s\n' ab cd abcd abcdab abx cdcdx x abxx abc ababcdcdabx axb | grep -f pattern-bre.txt; echo "matched: $(printf '%s\n' ab cd abcd abcdab abx cdcdx x abxx abc ababcdcdabx axb | grep -cf pattern-bre.txt)"
^(ab|cd)+x?$
#> out: matched: 0
#> out-not: matched: 6
#> check: pattern-bre.txt contains an unescaped ( | + or ?
#> check-not: PASS: day 13
